{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"factorization by inspection factorization by inspection","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md","fileName":"Factorization by Inspection.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Factorization by Inspection","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Factoring and Algebraic Identities]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Algebraic Identities","Polynomial Expansion","Completing the Square","Quadratic Formula","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"factorization by inspection factorization by inspection","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Factorization by Inspection.md","fileName":"Factorization by Inspection.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Factorization by Inspection","heading":"Factorization by Inspection","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Factorization by inspection searches possible factor pairs and tests which product reproduces a polynomial's coefficients. For a quadratic trinomial, signs and the factors of the leading and constant coefficients constrain the possible binomials.\n\nInspection is systematic trial rather than unsupported guessing. Each candidate is checked through [[Polynomial Expansion]], and symmetry can reduce the cases. It works efficiently when coefficients are small and integer factors exist. A quadratic that does not factor conveniently over integers may require [[Completing the Square]] or the [[Quadratic Formula]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Alge